1
00:00:07,820 --> 00:00:09,360
Rym: It's Tuesday, June 11th, 2024.

2
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:09,681
Rym: I'm Rym.

3
00:00:12,115 --> 00:00:12,570
Scott: I'm Scott.

4
00:00:12,830 --> 00:00:13,914
Rym: And this is GeekNights.

5
00:00:13,954 --> 00:00:19,290
Rym: Tonight we are reviewing the 2024 Metroidvania Animal Well.

6
00:00:20,350 --> 00:00:21,367
Scott: Let's do this.

7
00:00:22,611 --> 00:00:34,310
Rym: So, when you're a fan of a sports team that makes it deep into, say, a playoffs like the Stanley Cup, and then it gets eliminated, there's two different ways to look at how you feel about the team that eliminated you.

8
00:00:35,531 --> 00:00:40,690
Rym: Usually, you can either look at it from the perspective of, say they go on to win the cup.

9
00:00:41,370 --> 00:00:44,121
Rym: Then at least you can say, "Well, we got as far as we could.

10
00:00:44,141 --> 00:00:46,249
Rym: They were the ones who were going to win it

11
00:00:46,329 --> 00:00:46,450
Rym: anyway.".

12
00:00:47,131 --> 00:00:49,650
Scott: Well, that's just the nature of a single-elimination tournament.

13
00:00:49,890 --> 00:00:54,230
Scott: A single-elimination tournament only semi-ranks everyone.

14
00:00:54,390 --> 00:00:59,850
Scott: If you don't have third-place matches and all these extra matches, you don't really get a full ranking.

15
00:00:59,971 --> 00:01:03,728
Scott: You just know, like, "Well, I beat those three people, and then I

16
00:01:03,768 --> 00:01:04,108
Scott: lost.".

17
00:01:04,370 --> 00:01:05,950
Rym: And the seating matters so much.

18
00:01:06,010 --> 00:01:13,930
Scott: Therefore, I'm no worse than the third worst, because I know I'm better than those two, but I lost to them.

19
00:01:14,631 --> 00:01:23,310
Scott: So, it's possible that anyone that they beat could actually be in between me and the people who beat me, and I could be worse than all of them.

20
00:01:24,112 --> 00:01:28,010
Scott: And it's possible that I could be worse than every single team on the other side of the bracket.

21
00:01:28,812 --> 00:01:36,090
Scott: Or, if the team that beats me wins the whole thing, yeah, I could still be worse than all those teams.

22
00:01:36,371 --> 00:01:41,150
Scott: Or, I could literally be the second best, even though I didn't make it to the final round.

23
00:01:42,531 --> 00:01:45,169
Scott: And that single-elimination tournament isn't there.

24
00:01:46,893 --> 00:01:50,090
Scott: The purpose of it isn't to rank everyone from first to last accurately.

25
00:01:50,830 --> 00:01:56,210
Scott: The purpose of it is just we only care about first, and we don't care about any other ranking, because only first matters.

26
00:01:56,431 --> 00:02:00,309
Scott: And it accurately determines who is first.

27
00:02:01,871 --> 00:02:13,230
Rym: So, when I was in Quiz Bowl, like Varsity Quiz Bowl in high school, there was one tournament every year where there's this one Catholic school that would always be the number one seed, because they seeded based on other tournaments.

28
00:02:14,434 --> 00:02:16,469
Rym: And we could beat the shit out of them.

29
00:02:16,870 --> 00:02:29,650
Rym: So, we would specifically tank and try to come in last place in every tournament that counted for seeding to make sure we played them in the first round of this other tournament, and we'd always crush them.

30
00:02:29,670 --> 00:02:30,553
Scott: Wait, wait, wait, wait, wait.

31
00:02:30,613 --> 00:02:35,270
Scott: You tank, and then they match the two worst teams against each other?

32
00:02:35,470 --> 00:02:35,731
Rym: No, no, no.

33
00:02:35,812 --> 00:02:39,830
Rym: This team would always be seeded number one for varies like one or two.

34
00:02:39,930 --> 00:02:41,850
Rym: Like, they'd be near the top seed reliably.

35
00:02:42,313 --> 00:02:42,705
Scott: Oh, okay.

36
00:02:43,011 --> 00:02:47,850
Rym: We wanted to face them because we pretty much always beat them whenever we faced them, for whatever reason.

37
00:02:48,939 --> 00:02:49,050
Scott: Okay.

38
00:02:50,131 --> 00:02:59,090
Scott: So, we would tank on purpose, and I remember- Wouldn't it be better to win and then beat them in the finals?

39
00:02:59,450 --> 00:03:00,993
Rym: Well, because we often lost a lot of the other teams.

40
00:03:01,013 --> 00:03:01,695
Rym: We weren't the best.

41
00:03:01,755 --> 00:03:08,850
Rym: We were not great, but if we could win that first round, like getting past the first round, we often did pretty well.

42
00:03:08,930 --> 00:03:12,710
Scott: If you weren't great, how could this first ranked team reliably lose to you?

43
00:03:12,770 --> 00:03:20,630
Rym: Because of, I think, I mean, this is only over the course of like three years, so I have three years of bi-annual tournaments to go on.

44
00:03:21,170 --> 00:03:32,490
Rym: But, another factor in a single elimination tournament is the rock, paper, scissors of what if Rym is better than Scott at this game, but Scott is better than- Right, but this is a game of trivia.

45
00:03:33,133 --> 00:03:35,852
Rym: It is, but the tournament- It's just knowledge.

46
00:03:36,093 --> 00:03:39,040
Scott: It's like if they know, they'd either know more than you or less than you.

47
00:03:39,100 --> 00:03:56,830
Rym: It's not like there's- I guess different blind spots on Team A versus Team B. Our team was always really good at mythology and literature and European history, but we didn't know shit about architecture or art history.

48
00:03:58,071 --> 00:04:10,670
Rym: So, depending on what set of things the people on your team know versus the other team, and then depending on what is chosen to be the topics, which you often don't know in advance, but many tournaments tended to draw from similar pools.

49
00:04:12,231 --> 00:04:20,050
Rym: All I'm saying is they're gonna be a hockey team that's really good against one specific other hockey team, but is not the best hockey team otherwise by a wide margin.

50
00:04:21,470 --> 00:04:28,948
Rym: But, the other way to approach this is the team that beats you, you want to get their asses handed to them just to show them up like, "Well, I don't want you to win.

51
00:04:28,968 --> 00:04:29,469
Rym: You beat us.

52
00:04:29,549 --> 00:04:29,750
Rym: Fuck

53
00:04:29,790 --> 00:04:29,890
Rym: you.".

54
00:04:30,630 --> 00:04:32,475
Scott: Well, there's all sorts of motivations, right?

55
00:04:32,515 --> 00:04:38,530
Scott: So, take for example fans of the Calgary Flames, a team that did not make the playoffs this year, right?

56
00:04:39,191 --> 00:04:42,878
Scott: They are, you know, as much as New York Rangers and Islanders.

57
00:04:42,938 --> 00:04:49,730
Scott: fans hate each other, Calgary and Edmonton fans are the two hockey teams from the province of Alberta.

58
00:04:50,170 --> 00:04:50,892
Scott: They hate each other.

59
00:04:51,112 --> 00:04:52,977
Scott: They hate each other a lot, right?

60
00:04:53,398 --> 00:04:58,490
Scott: So, the Calgary fans really do not want Edmonton to win the Stanley Cup, right?

61
00:04:58,971 --> 00:05:13,690
Scott: However, you also have to consider that just a couple years ago, right, Calgary made a huge trade, right, with the Florida Panthers to send them Matthew Tkachuk, right?

62
00:05:14,050 --> 00:05:35,070
Scott: Which basically, a player who is like, you know, mega star caliber, right, in the NHL, who effectively that player sort of gave up on their team and didn't play, you know, the best he could have went to Florida and is now playing, you know, and now is doing much better.

63
00:05:36,772 --> 00:05:44,702
Scott: And so, yeah, they have very good reason to be incredibly upset and not want the Florida Panthers to succeed.

64
00:05:46,043 --> 00:05:49,167
Scott: And yet, here they are, and those are the only two possibilities.

65
00:05:49,488 --> 00:05:50,829
Scott: One of those teams will hoist.

66
00:05:51,630 --> 00:05:58,470
Scott: But no matter what, Calgary Flames fans are already buried in salt before it's even over.

67
00:05:58,790 --> 00:06:05,750
Rym: The Edmonton Oilers are already getting shut down very similarly to how the Rangers got shut down against this team.

68
00:06:06,211 --> 00:06:10,610
Scott: Well, they got more shots on the goalie than the Rangers did.

69
00:06:11,130 --> 00:06:12,956
Rym: But they're still getting shut down in the neutral zone.

70
00:06:12,976 --> 00:06:15,244
Rym: They're not like the top tier opportunities.

71
00:06:15,264 --> 00:06:16,890
Rym: I feel like Florida is going to win it.

72
00:06:17,912 --> 00:06:20,778
Scott: I mean, yeah, that's what everyone thought.

73
00:06:20,939 --> 00:06:25,649
Scott: I mean, it's still not guaranteed, but yeah, I wouldn't bet against it.

74
00:06:28,053 --> 00:06:30,290
Rym: So, yeah, all that aside, you got any news?

75
00:06:31,570 --> 00:06:33,193
Scott: Well, we knew this news already.

76
00:06:33,273 --> 00:06:42,590
Scott: This seems to be a pattern in my life now, where I read so much internet that, like, I know things, and then eventually they get announced officially.

77
00:06:43,372 --> 00:06:45,258
Scott: And it's like, it doesn't feel like it's news.

78
00:06:45,338 --> 00:06:47,806
Scott: It feels like someone's just repeating the news, right?

79
00:06:47,966 --> 00:06:48,748
Scott: Where it's like, "I already knew

80
00:06:48,789 --> 00:06:48,929
Scott: that.".

81
00:06:49,692 --> 00:06:52,423
Rym: Like, "Oh, there's gonna be a PAX East in the year

82
00:06:52,423 --> 00:06:54,233
Rym: 2028.".

83
00:06:54,233 --> 00:06:55,247
Scott: You don't say, right?

84
00:06:55,611 --> 00:06:57,390
Rym: It'll be sometime in the late spring.

85
00:06:58,370 --> 00:07:02,280
Scott: Yeah, but it's, you know, some months a while ago, right?

86
00:07:02,601 --> 00:07:04,746
Scott: We got wind that this was occurring.

87
00:07:04,786 --> 00:07:06,090
Scott: There was lots of rumblings.

88
00:07:06,491 --> 00:07:15,706
Scott: But now there is an official announcement and trailer from, I guess, the Summer Games Fest, which is some sort of, I don't know, game-announce-y thing.

89
00:07:15,766 --> 00:07:17,850
Scott: I don't even fully understand where it comes from.

90
00:07:18,050 --> 00:07:21,110
Rym: Uploaded to YouTube four days ago, coming in 2025.

91
00:07:21,110 --> 00:07:22,855
Scott: Yeah, it's Civilization VII.

92
00:07:22,935 --> 00:07:26,525
Scott: So, I mean, there's nothing else that needs to be said.

93
00:07:26,586 --> 00:07:27,428
Scott: We knew, right?

94
00:07:28,871 --> 00:07:30,938
Rym: I played every Civ a lot.

95
00:07:31,620 --> 00:07:34,450
Rym: If I had to rank them, I actually think VI is the best.

96
00:07:36,252 --> 00:07:36,813
Scott: I don't know about that.

97
00:07:36,834 --> 00:07:39,721
Scott: But anyway, the point is, what do we know about this game?

98
00:07:39,741 --> 00:07:43,050
Scott: I mean, who knows, right?

99
00:07:43,150 --> 00:07:48,609
Rym: Well, I would note there are, in the history of Civ, there have been two kinds of new Civs.

100
00:07:48,990 --> 00:07:56,610
Rym: There's the kind that takes the broad pattern of how the previous one went and refines it and shuffles it a little bit.

101
00:07:56,950 --> 00:08:02,290
Rym: But it's like, it's basically the same blueprint, but they've updated it and made it better in some way.

102
00:08:03,610 --> 00:08:06,275
Rym: Or they do something radical like, "Yeah, hexes!

103
00:08:06,736 --> 00:08:07,497
Rym: One unit per hex!

104
00:08:07,537 --> 00:08:07,918
Rym: Fuck you!

105
00:08:07,938 --> 00:08:14,049
Rym: We've completely changed a core aspect of the game absolutely and fundamentally in a way that's going to completely change how the game gets

106
00:08:14,089 --> 00:08:14,350
Rym: played.".

107
00:08:15,071 --> 00:08:16,650
Scott: We don't know which kind of Civ this is going to be.

108
00:08:16,670 --> 00:08:20,010
Rym: We never know what kind it's going to be until they announce, until they tell us how it works.

109
00:08:20,770 --> 00:08:27,930
Scott: But the point is, is that I'm worried that, you know, just because of the way, I don't have any specific information about this game.

110
00:08:28,010 --> 00:08:29,070
Rym: I think I have the same worry.

111
00:08:29,673 --> 00:08:33,169
Scott: Yeah, just, I know about how the industry and the world is going.

112
00:08:33,169 --> 00:08:35,590
Rym: I'm going to write something down and then I'm going to see what you say.

113
00:08:35,789 --> 00:08:39,870
Scott: Will this become microtransaction, gambling bullshit?

114
00:08:40,909 --> 00:08:45,010
Scott: Or will it still be a game that you buy once and you can play Civ to your heart's content?

115
00:08:46,771 --> 00:08:47,797
Scott: Because they could do that.

116
00:08:47,838 --> 00:08:48,904
Scott: They could make Civ pay to win.

117
00:08:48,924 --> 00:08:49,830
Scott: What are you going to do about it?

118
00:08:50,371 --> 00:08:53,670
Rym: Yeah, I mean, just continue to play Civ V and Civ VI forever.

119
00:08:54,893 --> 00:08:55,295
Scott: Yeah, okay.

120
00:08:55,416 --> 00:08:56,121
Scott: Or make our own.

121
00:08:56,282 --> 00:08:57,610
Scott: There are some open-source-y ones.

122
00:08:58,072 --> 00:09:03,410
Scott: But anyway, or the other question is, will they finally make a multiplayer function?

123
00:09:03,790 --> 00:09:04,658
Scott: There's no universe.

124
00:09:04,840 --> 00:09:05,889
Rym: They've never done it.

125
00:09:06,351 --> 00:09:26,690
Rym: Every time they have tried to make multiplayer work for real, they somehow fuck it up in like amazing ways to the fact that even in Civ VI, the latest and greatest, which has like asynchronous and synchronous multiplayer built in, in the UI is supposed to just work.

126
00:09:27,175 --> 00:09:28,127
Rym: One, it just fucking crashes.

127
00:09:30,951 --> 00:09:39,650
Scott: If I was them, what I would do is, you know, they're always trying to make money, at least with Civ VI, they tried to make money selling all these DLCs over time.

128
00:09:39,810 --> 00:09:43,790
Rym: I don't think I ever engaged with any expansion other than the core ones at any point.

129
00:09:44,730 --> 00:09:46,335
Scott: Other than the ones that give you.

130
00:09:46,396 --> 00:09:54,230
Scott: like the major expansions that like change the rules of the game and stuff, most of them are like scenarios for people who want to play single player.

131
00:09:55,034 --> 00:09:57,910
Rym: I gotta say, the scenarios in all Civ games.

132
00:09:58,150 --> 00:09:59,790
Scott: Or like extra leaders, you know.

133
00:09:59,950 --> 00:10:04,370
Rym: In my, I guess, "Gig Knights" technically makes money, so technically this is a professional opinion.

134
00:10:04,970 --> 00:10:11,130
Rym: In my professional opinion, no scenario in any Civilization game has ever been good.

135
00:10:12,010 --> 00:10:14,430
Scott: I mean, I don't know if they're good or not because I just don't care about them.

136
00:10:14,450 --> 00:10:15,037
Rym: People play them though.

137
00:10:15,118 --> 00:10:19,130
Scott: Like people seem to- Yeah, there are people who are interested in those, but I'm not.

138
00:10:21,034 --> 00:10:28,330
Rym: They just feel like an okay video game implemented in a mediocre way, like hacked into Civ.

139
00:10:28,570 --> 00:10:29,749
Rym: Like they don't feel like they're good.

140
00:10:30,510 --> 00:10:35,410
Scott: I'm not interested in like trying to recreate some real world, you know, real history thing.

141
00:10:35,530 --> 00:10:42,170
Rym: But they're never even recreating real history because they're so bound by the mechanics of the Civ game that they're never actually good or historic.

142
00:10:42,510 --> 00:10:45,090
Scott: Like they can't- I don't know if they're good or historic because I haven't played them.

143
00:10:45,150 --> 00:10:45,979
Rym: I've played a ton of them.

144
00:10:46,060 --> 00:10:46,627
Rym: I've tried them out.

145
00:10:47,111 --> 00:10:51,630
Rym: I stopped with six because everyone I've ever tried has been deeply unsatisfying.

146
00:10:52,270 --> 00:10:55,590
Scott: Okay, well I've never played any of those scenarios because I don't give a shit about scenarios.

147
00:10:55,671 --> 00:10:58,409
Rym: I played a ton of them in four and they were all so bad.

148
00:10:59,290 --> 00:11:12,010
Scott: Okay, well anyway, what they could do and should do in my opinion to sort of split the baby and make money is they should make Civ 7 multiplayer first.

149
00:11:13,132 --> 00:11:14,770
Scott: Don't disregard the single player.

150
00:11:15,371 --> 00:11:19,330
Scott: But Civ has always been single player first with multiplayer tacked on.

151
00:11:19,430 --> 00:11:22,710
Rym: For a lot of reasons because in the old days like multiplayer was impossible.

152
00:11:23,950 --> 00:11:32,530
Scott: I'm saying go multiplayer, make the game multiplayer first and then just program some AIs even if they're crappy ones that single player works.

153
00:11:32,912 --> 00:11:34,670
Scott: It's just the multiplayer game against computers.

154
00:11:35,271 --> 00:11:43,649
Scott: And then sell all sorts of cosmetic bullshit that I don't have to buy and I can ignore that other people will buy to fund the game.

155
00:11:45,738 --> 00:11:48,170
Scott: They could skin their units and such and such.

156
00:11:48,210 --> 00:11:52,790
Rym: I refuse to elaborate because it would take me quite a while to explain how this would work.

157
00:11:53,210 --> 00:12:04,810
Rym: But I am confident if they made a multiplayer first Civ that just worked that a significant and serious esports league could form around it and be popular and successful.

158
00:12:05,050 --> 00:12:07,330
Scott: It had to make the game more, you know, better.

159
00:12:09,456 --> 00:12:13,250
Rym: I think it would work but it's a radically different kind of esport.

160
00:12:13,410 --> 00:12:16,350
Rym: And again I'm unwilling to elaborate in the context of this episode.

161
00:12:16,770 --> 00:12:18,376
Scott: Well the problem is in Civ.

162
00:12:18,396 --> 00:12:21,870
Scott: they've never balanced it properly because it's not a game about balance.

163
00:12:22,431 --> 00:12:25,910
Scott: So you can't really have it be a competitive, a serious competitive thing.

164
00:12:25,910 --> 00:12:28,270
Rym: I don't think esports need to be fair.

165
00:12:29,190 --> 00:12:30,458
Scott: There's also a lot of vote.

166
00:12:30,498 --> 00:12:32,530
Scott: who wins in a Civ game with more than two players.

167
00:12:32,590 --> 00:12:36,530
Rym: I don't think they need to be fair or anything need to be serious but not fair.

168
00:12:36,570 --> 00:12:37,307
Scott: Which of a sport then?

169
00:12:38,491 --> 00:12:45,450
Rym: It's about as good a sport as the WWE and again I refuse to elaborate because it would take the entire episode.

170
00:12:46,190 --> 00:12:48,070
Scott: Yeah well no one wants that.

171
00:12:50,371 --> 00:12:56,130
Scott: Even people who play video games competitively seriously invest their time in them.

172
00:12:57,071 --> 00:13:02,748
Scott: They don't say the things that we say but they inherently understand the things that we say internally.

173
00:13:03,932 --> 00:13:07,925
Scott: And no one would invest the time to be competitive in a game.

174
00:13:07,986 --> 00:13:09,330
Scott: that wasn't, you know, at least.

175
00:13:09,651 --> 00:13:12,230
Scott: It's not like it has to be devoid of all luck, right?

176
00:13:12,290 --> 00:13:14,549
Scott: There's plenty of people playing competitive card games out there.

177
00:13:15,051 --> 00:13:18,270
Scott: But it has to be, you know, not Civ.

178
00:13:19,613 --> 00:13:22,070
Rym: I think it could work but again I refuse to elaborate.

179
00:13:22,532 --> 00:13:23,450
Rym: I would have to outright.

180
00:13:24,451 --> 00:13:28,890
Scott: You'd have to make Civ, you'd have to basically make it significantly different from the past.

181
00:13:29,351 --> 00:13:34,570
Scott: You'd have to have, you know, first of all you'd have to start with some sort of drafting or something to pick your leaders.

182
00:13:35,711 --> 00:13:38,190
Rym: Which frankly Civ fucking 6 should have.

183
00:13:38,650 --> 00:13:43,110
Scott: And then you'd have to eliminate the randomness of the starting position so you don't get hosed immediately.

184
00:13:44,110 --> 00:13:48,150
Scott: And then you'd have to write all the random events like oh yeah it just floods and kills you.

185
00:13:49,112 --> 00:13:52,830
Rym: I think actually you could keep all of those and you could make something that would be popular.

186
00:13:53,290 --> 00:13:55,570
Rym: But it would be popular with a different demographic.

187
00:13:55,912 --> 00:13:58,910
Rym: But to get away from all that because that's a whole different side thing.

188
00:13:59,853 --> 00:14:02,610
Rym: The thing I wrote down, the thing I'm actually worried about with this Civ.

189
00:14:03,170 --> 00:14:12,730
Rym: Is something that every modern Civ game has been literally not worth playing until at least one full on expansion has been released.

190
00:14:12,971 --> 00:14:13,629
Rym: And sometimes two.

191
00:14:15,253 --> 00:14:19,890
Rym: Like Civ 5 was a terrible game until they fully expanded it.

192
00:14:20,451 --> 00:14:23,230
Rym: Civ 4 was a terrible game until they fully expanded it.

193
00:14:23,511 --> 00:14:26,329
Rym: Civ 6 was not that fun until they expanded it.

194
00:14:26,871 --> 00:14:29,170
Rym: I'm not going to play Civ 7 for at least a year.

195
00:14:29,950 --> 00:14:30,393
Scott: Oh you think?

196
00:14:30,494 --> 00:14:31,179
Scott: Alright.

197
00:14:31,360 --> 00:14:32,770
Scott: That's a valid, entirely valid.

198
00:14:32,930 --> 00:14:37,990
Scott: I think that is partially a strategy for game design and development.

199
00:14:38,170 --> 00:14:40,150
Scott: And also a strategy for making money, right?

200
00:14:40,230 --> 00:14:48,010
Scott: Is that they put the game out and then it's like they finally get, you know, you're basically playtesting and seeing with these core systems.

201
00:14:48,450 --> 00:14:49,670
Scott: And they're seeing what's happening.

202
00:14:49,890 --> 00:14:51,690
Scott: They collect data from real players.

203
00:14:52,232 --> 00:14:56,350
Scott: And then an expansion is, they sell it as an expansion.

204
00:14:56,530 --> 00:14:58,369
