238
00:17:14,810 --> 00:17:27,290
Rym: But we made deals like, we're going to petition Scott, I'm going to take these cities, you take these cities, you stay the fuck out of this and don't tell Scott about the armada that you accidentally saw that's heading his way.

239
00:17:28,653 --> 00:17:31,530
Rym: Like that's real fun and that does not exist in single player Civ.

240
00:17:33,570 --> 00:17:42,850
Rym: And it's very, you know this is true because of the fact that there are so many third party things to facilitate multiplayer.

241
00:17:43,190 --> 00:17:55,830
Rym: Before Civ even allowed multiplayer, like even in Civ 2, people were literally passing save files around and just fucking hacking like asynchronous multiplayer in by emailing files to each other because of how fun it was.

242
00:17:59,441 --> 00:18:00,683
Rym: So I don't really have a news.

243
00:18:00,783 --> 00:18:09,220
Rym: I got a little bit of a geek bite in the sense that I, around the time of the pandemic when that started, started playing Blades in the Dark.

244
00:18:09,481 --> 00:18:14,900
Rym: One of many role playing systems that we have enjoyed and care a lot about and have talked about in the past.

245
00:18:15,441 --> 00:18:21,138
Rym: But I've actually kept playing that game every other week unless something major came up.

246
00:18:22,321 --> 00:18:31,200
Rym: Since then, to the point that I can say now that I have basically done the equivalent of play D&D from level 1 to level 20 in Blades in the Dark.

247
00:18:31,660 --> 00:18:41,120
Rym: Like it is rare as an adult that I have fully explored the systems of a role playing game end to end where like I know the book, everything.

248
00:18:41,881 --> 00:18:45,960
Rym: We're playing a crew that went from nothing to like tier 4, end game content.

249
00:18:46,360 --> 00:18:51,250
Rym: Unless we do some expansions and start making shit up, we run out of stuff to do mechanically.

250
00:18:52,601 --> 00:18:57,520
Rym: And as a result, I want to give a little review of Blades in the Dark as an end game system.

251
00:18:58,382 --> 00:19:04,340
Rym: And for one, this is now one of my top three role playing systems.

252
00:19:04,540 --> 00:19:11,680
Rym: Blades in the Dark gets past so many of the things that make other role playing games hard to play.

253
00:19:13,641 --> 00:19:19,700
Rym: The flashback mechanisms of flashing back to a plan instead of having a plan in advance.

254
00:19:20,280 --> 00:19:27,000
Rym: That alone, adding something like that to almost any other role playing game, indie or not, would just literally make it better.

255
00:19:27,501 --> 00:19:36,940
Rym: Like so much time gets wasted at the table with meta planning around systems instead of just doing something cool and then bringing a flashback in to figure out how you got there.

256
00:19:37,120 --> 00:19:38,138
Rym: Like skipping to the cool part.

257
00:19:39,761 --> 00:19:47,900
Rym: The dye pools, like the way the mechanics work for leveling up is just so simple and yet rich enough to give you a lot of variety in characters.

258
00:19:48,320 --> 00:20:03,580
Rym: Gets past the problem we've had with systems like say Burning Wheel, where as much as I love it, it's very hard to play a game like Burning Wheel with anyone who does not know the rules as well as you do, assuming that you know the rules very well.

259
00:20:04,820 --> 00:20:18,659
Rym: And the burden of playing most indie RPGs with someone who does not fully understand the rules and is not willing to learn them all, that burden is so high that I'm increasingly unwilling to do it.

260
00:20:19,241 --> 00:20:29,280
Rym: And yet Blades in the Dark just works with people who don't fucking know the rules at all and the whole table supports them almost effortlessly just by the nature of the mechanics.

261
00:20:31,481 --> 00:20:40,380
Rym: But the other thing that really stood out, having played all the way through to the end, is that Blades in the Dark does this balance that I think very few systems have done well.

262
00:20:41,141 --> 00:20:59,260
Rym: In that it includes a setting and the setting has just enough proper nouns and numbers to make it very easy to just pull things into the game and make them work and have them all be contextual without a game master having to write a grand opus.

263
00:20:59,280 --> 00:21:10,480
Rym: Because I'm sure we've all played a role-playing game where you've got the friend, like for us it was Alex, who will orchestrate the grand story of a custom world and play a campaign through it.

264
00:21:11,080 --> 00:21:21,420
Rym: But as we all know, there aren't that many humans on Earth who are willing and able to put that kind of effort in and a very small percentage of the humans who are willing to put in that effort are any good at it.

265
00:21:22,161 --> 00:21:28,516
Scott: And when you do play a game like that, how much are you the player really contributing?

266
00:21:28,536 --> 00:21:30,400
Scott: You're mostly just having someone tell you a story.

267
00:21:30,860 --> 00:21:34,630
Scott: If you're just having someone tell you a story, why are you playing a tabletop RPG?

268
00:21:34,650 --> 00:21:38,820
Scott: You could just have them tell you the story and furthermore you could just watch a movie.

269
00:21:39,820 --> 00:21:50,200
Scott: So the tabletop RPG means that it's not worth it unless everyone is contributing and creating something that no one person foresaw before they arrived.

270
00:21:50,561 --> 00:21:58,360
Rym: So right before I tried Blades in the Dark for the first time, I had been writing a campaign that I was going to run with some of our friends in Burning Rail.

271
00:21:59,140 --> 00:22:08,540
Rym: And the idea was like, I'll go a city, and you're thieves in a thieves guild, and thief shit happens, like run your thieves guild in this medieval city with a bunch of drama happening.

272
00:22:09,400 --> 00:22:11,986
Rym: And it took a long time to try to start putting this together.

273
00:22:12,086 --> 00:22:18,620
Rym: And then I played Blades in the Dark, and Blades in the Dark mechanically just makes that happen without the game master having to do fucking anything.

274
00:22:20,581 --> 00:22:49,356
Rym: Like, if I had to recommend a system, if you have a small group of friends you want to play roleplaying games with, and it's hard to get them going, Blades in the Dark might not be the best at this, but it is near the top of all the RPGs I have ever played in terms of the likelihood of getting a bunch of people with varying levels of experience with RPGs or even the system, saying let's play a game,

275
00:22:49,737 --> 00:22:49,977
Scott: and then

276
00:22:50,297 --> 00:22:52,620
Rym: actually getting past session zero and playing a session.

277
00:22:53,180 --> 00:22:57,579
Rym: Blades in the Dark I think has the highest success rate of almost any system I can think of.

278
00:22:58,742 --> 00:23:02,639
Rym: I think it'll do better than D&D, because D&D is really fucking complicated, actually.

279
00:23:05,861 --> 00:23:18,560
Rym: And I guess the final note I'd like to say is having played this system all the way through to its endgame now, it is surprising to me how radically different it plays just based on the numbers that change as you level up.

280
00:23:19,201 --> 00:23:21,407
Rym: Like, the game plays radically differently.

281
00:23:21,447 --> 00:23:26,300
Rym: It's almost like completely different games that just sort of stack on top of each other.

282
00:23:26,920 --> 00:23:44,100
Rym: Every one of them is different, and every one of them is equally compelling to the point that you kind of never get bored, and I think that is a consequence of the numbers in the system and the mechanics more so than is the consequence of anything any human playing the game is doing.

283
00:23:45,120 --> 00:24:00,420
Rym: So, I don't want to elaborate super deep, I feel like this is more of a deep dive topic for another time, but my Geek Byte is, if you haven't played Blades in the Dark, ten out of ten, go play, use this system to play RPGs with your friends.

284
00:24:01,681 --> 00:24:08,639
Rym: I can think of very few games I'd recommend more highly than Blades in the Dark at this time, knowing what I know about it.

285
00:24:13,910 --> 00:24:16,372
Rym: But anyway, things of the day!

286
00:24:16,793 --> 00:24:18,354
Rym: So, a few things people don't know.

287
00:24:18,895 --> 00:24:37,590
Rym: One thing you might not know, if you see a big antenna, if that antenna is many things, like one of those big tower antennas you see up on a mountain, if you climb up there and you see one of those antennas, a lot of them, if you walk over and touch the bottom of that big structure, you'll probably be fine.

288
00:24:37,910 --> 00:24:43,376
Rym: Because it's like FM or something, and that tower is there mostly, so the antenna can be even higher.

289
00:24:44,097 --> 00:24:56,190
Rym: But a lot of people don't know that AM, like AM radio, amplitude modulation radio, the old school stuff, that structure, the entire thing is the antenna.

290
00:24:56,630 --> 00:25:00,495
Rym: It's not a tower to put an antenna on top of, it is an antenna.

291
00:25:01,256 --> 00:25:10,826
Rym: And two, the way AM works, if you even get close to that antenna, there's a good chance it's going to kill you, because of the sheer amount of electricity that is coursing through it.

292
00:25:11,147 --> 00:25:14,230
Rym: That whole thing is electrified to an insane degree.

293
00:25:16,291 --> 00:25:22,715
Rym: But, the other cool thing is that AM radio is amplitude modulation.

294
00:25:23,116 --> 00:25:31,262
Rym: And what that means is that the amplitude of the radio frequency signal is itself the data.

295
00:25:31,762 --> 00:25:41,810
Rym: It gets louder or softer at a ratio to how much strong or weak the RF signal itself is, like it's pulsing almost.

296
00:25:42,671 --> 00:25:56,126
Rym: So if you, say, put a hot dog on the end of a stick, and you ground that hot dog, and you touch that hot dog to an AM antenna, you will hear fucking radio coming out of the hot dog as the hot dog disintegrates.

297
00:25:57,588 --> 00:25:59,810
Rym: And this video is exactly that, and it's super cool.

