Scott: You're Samus is constantly every screen, almost there's at least boring bad guys to shoot and get in the way and damage you and kill you.

386
00:32:06,662 --> 00:32:09,245
Scott: Um, you know, Castlevania even more.

387
00:32:09,306 --> 00:32:11,889
Scott: So it's like, you're constantly whipping bats nonstop.

388
00:32:12,270 --> 00:32:12,430
Scott: Right.

389
00:32:12,770 --> 00:32:18,677
Scott: And in some cases, you know, that is actually, you know, good, good gaming, right?

390
00:32:18,737 --> 00:32:22,802
Scott: There's some sort of like, you know, you're, you're sort of getting two games mixed together, you're exploring.

391
00:32:23,122 --> 00:32:30,390
Scott: And then occasionally you're stopping to play sort of an action game, you know, shoot the guy in the weak spot, figure out how to beat the enemy.

392
00:32:30,670 --> 00:32:31,971
Scott: What weapons do I use?

393
00:32:31,991 --> 00:32:33,112
Scott: How do I navigate this?

394
00:32:33,813 --> 00:32:40,639
Scott: Um, you know, it's additional things to do, especially in like Metroid Dread, where you got the, the Emmy thing and figure out how to get away from it.

395
00:32:40,719 --> 00:32:40,859
Scott: Right.

396
00:32:41,240 --> 00:32:52,470
Scott: It's not that those components don't add anything to the game or that they should be eliminated totally, but a lot of times, if you think about it, what do those things add most of the time?

397
00:32:52,490 --> 00:32:58,298
Scott: Like how often is like, you watch Metroid speed runners, it's like, they just run through the room and ignore the bad guys.

398
00:32:58,358 --> 00:33:00,682
Scott: It's like, they're just in the way you hope you don't bump into them.

399
00:33:01,102 --> 00:33:01,322
Scott: Right.

400
00:33:01,763 --> 00:33:06,650
Scott: Or, you know, a casual player will go into a room and just shoot all the bad guys until they're dead.

401
00:33:07,231 --> 00:33:11,177
Scott: And it's like, it's sort of, you know, mindless, like just pee, pee, pee, pee, pee, pee, pee.

402
00:33:11,217 --> 00:33:18,370
Rym: Like you're, it's not really, it's like, it would be fine if the progression I had with Metroid specifically was when I first started playing the game.

403
00:33:18,751 --> 00:33:19,793
Rym: I'd kill every bad guy.

404
00:33:19,834 --> 00:33:21,197
Rym: Cause that's just what you do in video games.

405
00:33:21,598 --> 00:33:26,029
Rym: And the longer I played the game, the more I just got annoyed by them and just tried to avoid them.

406
00:33:28,290 --> 00:33:40,530
Scott: It's like, yeah, you know, what would it be so bad if we removed that part of the game, the sort of mindless, constant, just combat and, you know, and, and focus more on the other parts of the game.

407
00:33:41,091 --> 00:33:42,553
Scott: And so that's what animal well does.

408
00:33:42,613 --> 00:33:44,936
Scott: is there isn't really combat.

409
00:33:45,157 --> 00:33:51,586
Scott: and in the way that there is in a Metroid or a Castlevania, um, there aren't like there might, there are things that damage you.

410
00:33:51,826 --> 00:33:54,330
Scott: You can die, uh, like on spikes.

411
00:33:54,931 --> 00:33:58,720
Scott: Uh, and there are things that you can get touched by that will hurt, damage you.

412
00:33:58,740 --> 00:34:00,084
Scott: You lose your hit points.

413
00:34:00,104 --> 00:34:00,485
Scott: You'll die.

414
00:34:00,505 --> 00:34:02,510
Scott: You'll go back to the last save point you made.

415
00:34:03,211 --> 00:34:12,460
Scott: Um, and there's little telephones that you can make a call on to save the game, but you don't shoot the bad guys or whip them or attack them directly.

416
00:34:13,022 --> 00:34:20,230
Scott: Um, you know, you, they're just sort of part, they're really just part of the obstacles, part of the puzzle, um, of any particular room.

417
00:34:20,831 --> 00:34:22,233
Scott: How do I get from here to there?

418
00:34:22,672 --> 00:34:33,810
Scott: You know, how do I, uh, without them, you know, killing me or, uh, how can I, you know, use these bad guys to, to get the puzzle solved, to open the door or whatever I got to do.

419
00:34:33,870 --> 00:34:43,944
Rym: And I think that's where it sort of distilled a core component of a Metroidvania, which is not, not this combat piece, but just the, you're thinking, how do I get to there from here?

420
00:34:44,605 --> 00:34:45,786
Rym: How do I find a thing?

421
00:34:45,887 --> 00:34:47,007
Rym: Where am I going next?

422
00:34:47,369 --> 00:34:48,370
Rym: Is there a secret here?

423
00:34:48,693 --> 00:34:50,409
Rym: Like the game is basically just that.

424
00:34:53,351 --> 00:35:04,110
Scott: Uh, the other thing about this game is that, you know, the typical pattern in all these sorts of games is, you know, any explorer exploring a map game is that you're sort of gated, right?

425
00:35:04,770 --> 00:35:10,820
Scott: Zelda's are the, are the Kings of this also Metroids where you start the game with few things, not a lot of items.

426
00:35:11,281 --> 00:35:16,390
Scott: And because you don't have a lot of items, you could only get to a certain amount of the map.

427
00:35:16,871 --> 00:35:23,270
Scott: And many areas of the map are inaccessible to you until you get, say, I don't know, a hook shot or a double job.

428
00:35:23,771 --> 00:35:34,730
Scott: The power gloves, you can pick up the different colored rock that's in your way, a gravity suit, a various suit, you know, any of these sorts of items that will then let you go to a new place, explore a new area.

429
00:35:35,111 --> 00:35:39,479
Scott: And that sort of guides you into doing the game in a certain order, right?

430
00:35:39,579 --> 00:35:44,990
Scott: Following roughly a certain path of progression towards the final boss animal.

431
00:35:46,572 --> 00:35:51,004
Scott: You can explore basically the entire map from the get go.

432
00:35:51,145 --> 00:35:52,890
Scott: There's nothing really stopping you.

433
00:35:53,670 --> 00:36:06,430
Scott: There are some things that are extremely difficult to achieve or impossible without certain items, but there is a set of base items that if you can, if you just keep exploring, like you'll just get them in whatever order.

434
00:36:06,511 --> 00:36:08,830
Scott: Like I got them in completely different orders than other people.

435
00:36:09,792 --> 00:36:12,710
Rym: I got the firecracker first, then I got the disc.

436
00:36:12,951 --> 00:36:14,690
Scott: Well, everyone's going to get the firecracker.

437
00:36:14,811 --> 00:36:16,227
Rym: I hope you're going to get the firecracker first.

438
00:36:17,010 --> 00:36:20,700
Scott: That's sort of, because there is a starting area you have to get out of.

439
00:36:21,703 --> 00:36:22,967
Scott: I don't think there's any avoiding that.

440
00:36:23,007 --> 00:36:24,210
Scott: There might be that I don't know about.

441
00:36:24,330 --> 00:36:31,386
Rym: I don't want to take a moment that the firecracker effect is absolutely stunning in this game.

442
00:36:31,787 --> 00:36:32,829
Rym: It just looks so good.

443
00:36:35,671 --> 00:36:38,461
Scott: But after that, it's like, yeah, you could Rym got the disc first.

444
00:36:38,561 --> 00:36:40,989
Scott: I got the, the bee wand first, you know?

445
00:36:43,031 --> 00:36:47,963
Scott: And so, you know, these base items that like you're gonna get, right?

446
00:36:48,043 --> 00:36:50,950
Scott: That are just sort of, you can reach them directly from the start.

447
00:36:51,311 --> 00:36:54,303
Scott: They are kind of all you really need to beat the game.

448
00:36:54,704 --> 00:36:56,190
Scott: It's one of the, it's a lot like Super Metroid.

449
00:36:56,230 --> 00:36:57,694
Scott: There are these four flames.

450
00:36:57,735 --> 00:36:59,941
Scott: You can see them on the map when you start basically.

451
00:37:00,362 --> 00:37:03,030
Scott: And if you get those, the final area will unlock.

452
00:37:03,391 --> 00:37:06,490
Scott: You can go there and beat in quotes the final boss.

453
00:37:06,570 --> 00:37:07,996
Rym: I didn't even go for those right away.

454
00:37:08,177 --> 00:37:11,350
Rym: I just started looking for eggs and I found a lot of eggs.

455
00:37:11,910 --> 00:37:12,512
Scott: Yeah, that's it.

456
00:37:12,532 --> 00:37:16,822
Scott: You don't have to do the eggs, but you can and you maybe should, but you don't have to.

457
00:37:17,704 --> 00:37:19,448
Scott: You'll be rewarded for it if you do.

458
00:37:21,311 --> 00:37:24,639
Scott: But yeah, it's like, that's not all there is to the game, right?

459
00:37:24,799 --> 00:37:28,930
Scott: I beat the game by getting the four flames and go into the end.

460
00:37:29,050 --> 00:37:32,882
Scott: And it took me, like I said, two days on a weekend, a few hours each day.

461
00:37:33,404 --> 00:37:35,150
Scott: And then I watched some streamers play the game.

462
00:37:35,611 --> 00:37:41,630
Scott: And like they had ridiculous items that I never saw because they were actually trying to be more completionist, right?

463
00:37:41,791 --> 00:37:43,015
Scott: And not just go straight to the end.

464
00:37:44,320 --> 00:37:45,424
Scott: They were finding shit.

465
00:37:45,564 --> 00:37:46,548
Scott: I didn't even know items.

466
00:37:47,250 --> 00:37:48,835
Scott: I never even realized existed.

467
00:37:49,217 --> 00:37:53,350
Scott: Imagine like beating a Zelda game and like there was a hook shot.

468
00:37:53,670 --> 00:37:57,680
Scott: You just beat Ganon without ever getting the hook shot without knowing it existed.

469
00:37:58,081 --> 00:38:01,970
Scott: There were whole areas and places you could go and things you could get with the hook shot.

470
00:38:02,070 --> 00:38:05,502
Scott: You just never saw that part of the game because it wasn't required to beat the game.

471
00:38:06,947 --> 00:38:07,870
Scott: But yet you beat the game.

472
00:38:08,411 --> 00:38:09,736
Scott: And that's what this game is like.

473
00:38:09,797 --> 00:38:13,170
Scott: If I go back to it, there's a ton of shit I can get.

474
00:38:13,330 --> 00:38:16,998
Scott: Like maybe I haven't even done half the game probably.

475
00:38:17,058 --> 00:38:18,742
Scott: I felt like I did when I beat it.

476
00:38:19,203 --> 00:38:22,230
Scott: I had so much of the map uncovered and I had all these items.

477
00:38:22,530 --> 00:38:25,356
Scott: I felt like I had done 75% of the game.

478
00:38:25,697 --> 00:38:28,663
Scott: I probably did more like 40 if that, right?

479
00:38:29,464 --> 00:38:32,330
Scott: Even though the entire map almost was visible.

480
00:38:32,890 --> 00:38:39,750
Rym: Yeah, then I spent more hours playing than Scott and I basically got all these eggs and uncovered all this like cool secret stuff.

481
00:38:39,750 --> 00:38:40,592
Scott: I got a lot of eggs.

482
00:38:40,633 --> 00:38:43,220
Scott: I opened up two out of the four egg doors.

483
00:38:43,642 --> 00:38:46,209
Scott: But to get the fourth egg door, I think you need every egg.

484
00:38:48,074 --> 00:38:50,850
Rym: I haven't read anything about the parts I haven't encountered yet.

485
00:38:53,191 --> 00:38:54,754
Scott: Yeah.

486
00:38:54,814 --> 00:38:58,619
Scott: If you beat the game, then I guess you can check.

487
00:38:58,900 --> 00:39:05,710
Scott: But it's shocking just how much stuff you may have missed that you didn't realize you missed.

488
00:39:06,151 --> 00:39:16,730
Rym: But in terms of like the actual puzzles and design, because the puzzles in this game are very well designed in that you can logic your way through them pretty well.

489
00:39:17,290 --> 00:39:18,212
Rym: A lot of them are clever.

490
00:39:18,613 --> 00:39:26,790
Rym: The game has a lot of mechanics to where there are often three or four different ways to get past the same obstacle.

491
00:39:26,890 --> 00:39:28,395
Rym: And they're all kind of equally valid.

492
00:39:29,257 --> 00:39:33,370
Rym: And there might be an obstacle or a puzzle where there's three or four different ways to do it.

493
00:39:33,711 --> 00:39:36,865
Rym: And yet you'll just feel stupid for a while like, "How do I get up there?

494
00:39:36,906 --> 00:39:37,649
Rym: There's no way to get up

495
00:39:37,669 --> 00:39:37,810
Rym: there!".

496
00:39:37,890 --> 00:39:40,289
Rym: And then like you realize something obvious and then you can get up there.

497
00:39:41,050 --> 00:39:48,830
Rym: The game also does a really good job of showing you where there is something to interact with and guiding you toward solving puzzles.

498
00:39:49,410 --> 00:39:53,549
Rym: Without doing the thing that a lot of less well designed Metroid voices that have puzzles do.

499
00:39:55,031 --> 00:39:59,690
Rym: Where if you go where the game's guiding you, it kind of just naturally solves everything because it's right in your path.

500
00:40:00,091 --> 00:40:05,310
Rym: Some of the puzzles require surprisingly oblique strategies.

501
00:40:05,850 --> 00:40:12,810
Rym: Like, let me go into this area from the other side and try to fall in from the top and then I can do a thing because there's like a fish head in there or something.

502
00:40:15,431 --> 00:40:17,076
Rym: Some of the puzzles are really funny too.

503
00:40:17,116 --> 00:40:21,570
Rym: I think the moment where I laughed out loud, like literally laughed out loud, I had to pause the game.

504
00:40:22,871 --> 00:40:27,070
Rym: It's like not really a spoiler, but so early in the game, the first item I got was this disc.

505
00:40:27,130 --> 00:40:28,213
Rym: But you see this shrine.

506
00:40:28,935 --> 00:40:29,738
Rym: You got like nothing.

507
00:40:29,999 --> 00:40:33,790
Rym: You see this shrine, it's like a big wolf head and there's a disc in it, like a frisbee.

508
00:40:34,491 --> 00:40:41,129
Rym: If you take the disc, a giant ghost wolf pops out and starts chasing you and if it grabs you, it'll kill you.

509
00:40:41,750 --> 00:40:43,600
Rym: So you think, "Okay, I'll run away with this item.

510
00:40:43,821 --> 00:40:44,786
Rym: I clearly need this

511
00:40:44,887 --> 00:40:45,047
Rym: item.".

512
00:40:45,851 --> 00:40:47,036
Rym: That wolf doesn't go away.

513
00:40:47,659 --> 00:40:50,310
Rym: You can't just play the whole game with that wolf following you.

514
00:40:50,692 --> 00:40:51,829
Rym: That is not going to work.

515
00:40:53,010 --> 00:40:55,628
Rym: So, eventually I realize, "Alright, I can't do anything with

516
00:40:55,668 --> 00:40:55,809
Rym: this.".

517
00:40:56,253 --> 00:40:57,748
Rym: And I put the disc back and like, "Okay,

518
00:40:57,768 --> 00:40:57,950
Rym: whatever.".

519
00:40:58,551 --> 00:41:03,730
Rym: I keep going through the game and then I find the M disc, the mock disc.

520
00:41:04,410 --> 00:41:10,465
Rym: And obviously, I'm going to take that mock disc and Indiana Jones it with the disc.

521
00:41:10,846 --> 00:41:12,270
Rym: so the wolf spirit doesn't chase me.

522
00:41:12,410 --> 00:41:14,056
Rym: Like that makes sense to me logically.

523
00:41:14,076 --> 00:41:16,545
Rym: I go back and do it and yeah, now I got the disc.

524
00:41:16,826 --> 00:41:17,690
Rym: Wolf's not chasing me.

525
00:41:18,393 --> 00:41:20,160
Rym: Fast forward about three hours later.

526
00:41:20,180 --> 00:41:22,370
Rym: I am in a completely different part of the game.

527
00:41:22,550 --> 00:41:23,514
Rym: I've forgotten about that.

528
00:41:23,534 --> 00:41:24,820
Rym: Not even thinking about that anymore.

529
00:41:25,363 --> 00:41:26,930
Rym: Way off in the game going after a thing.

530
00:41:27,490 --> 00:41:34,150
Rym: I see a shrine and it's called, instead of the disc shrine, it's called the M disc shrine.

531
00:41:34,771 --> 00:41:41,790
Rym: It wants me to put the mock disc in it and now I got to go back and deal with that wolf spirit.

532
00:41:42,431 --> 00:41:47,369
Rym: And like the nature of that puzzle was just indicative of how this game goes.

533
00:41:49,092 --> 00:41:50,437
Rym: Also, a shout out to the slinky.

534
00:41:50,478 --> 00:41:51,482
Rym: I think we both agree.

535
00:41:51,502 --> 00:41:53,410
Rym: The slinky in this game.

536
00:41:54,530 --> 00:42:02,050
Rym: The sheer variety of puzzles that they make you solve using that slinky is possibly legendary.

537
00:42:02,850 --> 00:42:05,523
Rym: That slinky is used in so many clever ways.

538
00:42:05,563 --> 00:42:06,970
Rym: My hat is off to this designer.

539
00:42:07,630 --> 00:42:14,430
Scott: Yeah, I think that in a lot of games, you'll have some items that are used very frequently all over the place.

540
00:42:14,851 --> 00:42:20,590
Scott: They're like super useful and some items that like get used only when the game like has a specific slot.

541
00:42:20,830 --> 00:42:25,007
Scott: Like it's time to use that one item and there's maybe like 10 places that it interacts with.

542
00:42:25,067 --> 00:42:25,509
Scott: and that's it.

543
00:42:26,031 --> 00:42:33,470
Scott: Whereas in this game, most of the items are useful in a lot of places in a lot of different ways.

544
00:42:34,872 --> 00:42:39,509
Scott: And you can get really, you could actually get really creative and not completely.

545
00:42:40,372 --> 00:42:45,750
Scott: It's not completely a physics based way like Tears of the Kingdom, but it's like it's actually on that path, right?

546
00:42:45,830 --> 00:42:49,181
Scott: It's somewhere in between a Metroid and a Tears of the Kingdom.

547
00:42:49,261 --> 00:42:51,890
Rym: in terms of what if I dropped a bubble and jumped on the bubble?

548
00:42:52,290 --> 00:42:56,950
Rym: But then I like I throw a disc and I wait for it to bounce and I jump on the disc and then right now I'm cooking.

549
00:42:57,570 --> 00:43:02,350
Scott: Because of the way the items work, you can get really creative.

550
00:43:02,591 --> 00:43:09,270
Scott: And I watched people much better than me like get in secrets like the way that was clearly not intended from the back.

551
00:43:09,471 --> 00:43:13,230
Scott: Like navigating through spike tunnels that were clearly the exit.

552
00:43:13,450 --> 00:43:15,370
Scott: It's like is that the exit to the secret or the entrance?

553
00:43:15,450 --> 00:43:16,839
Scott: And it's like it doesn't matter.

554
00:43:16,899 --> 00:43:18,690
Scott: You can get through somehow, right?

555
00:43:19,090 --> 00:43:23,990
Scott: There's you can clearly see like when you get certain things like that there was a designed way.

556
00:43:24,270 --> 00:43:32,350
Scott: Like, ah, yes, this you'll if you play the game according to the logic of the creator, you will, you know, go here and then fall down this big pit.

557
00:43:32,430 --> 00:43:44,870
Scott: But actually you could climb up that pit if you did things a certain way and go into a thing from the back door that you weren't supposed to like, you know, was it intended or was it not?

558
00:43:44,990 --> 00:43:45,151
Scott: Right.

559
00:43:45,211 --> 00:43:51,350
Scott: It's sort of on the one hand feels unintended because there's an easier way to do it from the other side.

560
00:43:51,811 --> 00:43:52,072
Scott: Right.

561
00:43:52,132 --> 00:43:53,297
Scott: That was sort of meant to be.

562
00:43:53,679 --> 00:43:56,330
Scott: But on the other hand, there's so many things like that.

563
00:43:56,490 --> 00:44:01,330
Scott: It's like they designed it on purpose to let you go into back doors.

564
00:44:01,591 --> 00:44:08,710
Scott: If you are clever, lots of ways to cheese and, you know, do things in a non intended way.

565
00:44:09,111 --> 00:44:09,251
Scott: Right.

566
00:44:09,351 --> 00:44:21,050
Scott: I saw somebody basically there was a. there's a part in the game where there's a boss and you're supposed to drop the boss into this hole and it's going to hit these switches when it falls in the hole.

567
00:44:21,191 --> 00:44:23,729
Scott: And that's going to and that's going to unlock something.

568
00:44:24,993 --> 00:44:31,670
Scott: I saw somebody basically use some items cleverly to hit all the switches without ever going to the boss and unlock.

569
00:44:32,490 --> 00:44:43,050
Scott: That would be like if you went into a Zelda one dungeon and you got the door to open behind, you know, the boss and went in and grabbed the triforce piece and never grabbed the triforce leave.

570
00:44:43,673 --> 00:44:45,067
Scott: That's basically what the person did.

571
00:44:45,148 --> 00:44:45,330
Scott: Right.

572
00:44:45,350 --> 00:44:51,929
Scott: It's like the game is programmed in such a way that it's just the systems follow the rules of how they work.

573
00:44:53,410 --> 00:45:09,190
Scott: And while there is an obvious designed way to to solve, in quotes, every room and every secret and every puzzle and get every egg, you if you can make the systems work a different way, the game lets you encourages you and rewards you.

574
00:45:09,993 --> 00:45:11,002
Scott: Yeah, it doesn't care.

575
00:45:11,123 --> 00:45:11,930
Scott: It's like, go right ahead.

576
00:45:11,950 --> 00:45:13,033
Scott: Yeah.

577
00:45:13,293 --> 00:45:20,189
Rym: And the fact that you can basically if you when you start the game, if you walk in literally any direction, you will find interesting things to do.

578
00:45:21,451 --> 00:45:29,430
Rym: I think if you took a map of like how different people played through this game the first time, I think almost everyone did everything in a completely different order.

579
00:45:32,161 --> 00:45:38,795
Rym: I think the game also does a really good job specifically of letting you know there is there is a way past this or there isn't a way past this.

580
00:45:41,400 --> 00:45:43,707
Rym: Or in the very least, there isn't a normal way past this.

581
00:45:43,767 --> 00:45:48,200
Rym: Like you never are banging your head against something that is actually impossible.

582
00:45:48,320 --> 00:45:52,920
Rym: The game does a good job of making clear what is impossible or what you really should think is impossible.

583
00:45:55,721 --> 00:45:56,983
